  "notes": "A compound godan verb (く row) from {下|した} (below) + {向|む}く (to face). Describes the physical action of looking downward, which often carries emotional connotations such as embarrassment, sadness, or contemplation.\n\nUSAGE:\nThe て form {下向|したむ}いて and the たまま form {下向|したむ}いたまま are especially common, describing someone maintaining a downward gaze.\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- {下向|したむ}いたまま — while looking down\n- {下向|したむ}いて{歩|ある}く — to walk looking down\n- {下向|したむ}いて{黙|だま}る — to look down and stay silent\n\nSIMILAR WORDS:\n- {俯|うつむ}く — to look down, to cast one's eyes down; largely synonymous and slightly more literary\n- {下|した}を{向|む}く — the same meaning expressed as a phrase (particle を inserted); interchangeable in most contexts",
